## The moment

**"Go closer until the difference stops being a claim."**

A buyer comparing £280 against £56 cannot tell them apart at the size a product photo is printed — a 345mm knife across 816px is four pixels per millimetre. Everything the price is actually buying (forge scale, a hand-ground bevel, the wandering line where hard core meets soft iron) lives below that threshold. So the route's one moment is the thing a photograph cannot do: the reader walks up to the blade. One scroll-driven camera travels **×1 → ×12** into the knife, crossing from a whole-blade frame into a real macro of *the same knife*, and stops on the grind line. A live `LIFE SIZE ×n` readout counts it.

## What it moves

Two real photographs, both of one Moritaka Hamono kurouchi gyuto, both by Frank Schulenburg, both CC BY-SA 4.0 from Wikimedia Commons — a whole-knife frame (5618×3160) and a macro of its heel (6000×4000). Same knife, same photographer, same session.

Treated, not just resized: the macro is **mirrored and rotated −25°** so the blade sits on the same axis and orientation as the wide frame, then cropped, and both graded to one look (contrast, desaturation, colour split, vignette, unsharp) so stone and wood read as one shoot. Shipped as four webp derivatives (5000/2200 wide, 3400/1200 macro) plus `public/media/CREDITS.md` recording sources, licences, the exact ffmpeg treatment, and the registration constants.

## What drives it

One authored value in `src/blade.js`. Scroll progress → an eased exponent → a camera (a point in macro-normalised space plus a zoom). Everything reads from it:

- The two layers' transforms, tied by a measured **5.0× gain** and a shared physical anchor — the bottom corner of the heel, present in both frames.
- The handoff. The wide carries the picture to about ×4.4; then the macro **comes up through the middle of it** behind a radial mask that opens as it takes over, so the picture resolves rather than cuts. The threshold is set by resolution, not taste: below that scale the macro cannot fill the plate.
- The plate itself, held at 816px — exactly what 5618px of source can honestly show at 6.5× — then opening to full bleed once the macro has pixels to spare.
- Three annotations at coordinates I confirmed by drawing markers onto the shipped file and looking: the grind line, the forge scale, the machi. Each is placed by the same camera, so it stays on its feature at every zoom, flips to whichever side of its pin has room, and appears one at a time.

Reduced motion gets an authored still at ×11.8, ordered claim → picture → label → price. On a phone the pin stays on the blade and its text moves to a caption under the band.

## What I could not get

- **The two frames have different grounds and different perspective.** Stone under the wide, wood under the macro; the bevel reads slightly wider in the macro because the camera was closer. I matched orientation, axis, anchor and grade, and timed the handoff to where the blade fills most of the frame — but it is a dissolve between two photographs, not one continuous optical move. No available source had both scales on one ground.
- **The wide asset is 2.4MB.** At 6.5× it needs the pixels. It is preloaded and desktop-only (2200px on mobile), but it is heavy. A tiled zoom would halve it.
- **At DPR 2 the wide will be soft through the handoff.** There is no more source; 5618px is what was photographed.
- **No patina progression.** The strongest version of the £280 argument is the steel darkening over weeks — the page's own copy promises it. Two registered states of one blade do not exist in any free source I searched (Openverse across CC0/PD/BY/BY-SA, Wikimedia Commons across seven queries). The one candidate set was `by-nc-nd`, unusable commercially.
- **No interaction baseline yet** — this phase has no controls to give hover, focus and press states to. That is phase 2's first job, not something to defer past it.

